\paragraph{Encoder-conditional advantage.}
Part of the observed advantage may reflect text encoder quality rather than hidden-state informational superiority. At matched 384d dimensionality via PCA (79--92\% variance retained), the precision advantage persists on all four models (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all four model-level precision tests pass Holm-Bonferroni), eliminating capacity gap as the sole explanation. PCA retains directions of maximum variance in hidden-state space, which need not align with the semantic dimensions captured by trained text encoders, so this control is necessary but not sufficient. A stronger 1024d encoder closes most of the significance gap (1/4 HB-significant), suggesting a continuum rather than a binary modality distinction. The HS advantage has two separable components: a robust modality effect that survives dimensionality matching, and an encoder-dependent magnitude component that narrows with stronger baselines.

\paragraph{Cross-model divergence within identical architecture.}

\paragraph{CP annotation uncertainty.}
CP annotations follow the HarmThoughts methodology~\citep{harmthoughts}, which defines commitment points through content analysis of reasoning steps. We do not independently evaluate inter-annotator agreement (IAA). If CP annotation error is ${\geq}2$ steps, the reported lead times (+4.5 to +13 steps) may partially fall within annotation noise, particularly at the lower end of this range. Future work should quantify annotation reliability and assess its impact on probe evaluation.

